---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "manage",
 "name": "Google Cloud DNS",
 "title": "How to Add and Manage DNS Records in Google Cloud DNS",
 "description": "Add, edit, and delete DNS records in Google Cloud DNS step by step, with the gotchas that break sites and mail, and free tools to verify every change.",
 "intro": "Managing DNS in Google Cloud DNS is a short walk once you know where the records live and which defaults to watch. This is the complete flow, with the verification step most guides skip.",
 "panel": "Google Cloud console, Network Services, Cloud DNS: zones list, open a zone to manage its record sets.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open the zone",
   "Console, Cloud DNS, click the public zone."
  ],
  [
   "Add record set",
   "Add record set: DNS name, resource record type, TTL, and data. Google groups values per name+type like Azure."
  ],
  [
   "Verify",
   "Record lookup confirms the values; changes serve from Google's anycast network rapidly."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Zones live inside a GCP project: people edit the wrong project's zone constantly. Also the registrar-setup block is the authoritative NS list, not guesswork.",
  "fieldMap": { "name": "DNS name", "value": "Data", "ttl": "Default 300 s", "apex": "Zone apex entered as the zone origin", "note": "Google Cloud DNS manages records through record sets in each managed zone." },
"faqs": [
  [
   "Pricing?",
   "Per managed zone and per million queries; free tier covers modest traffic. Billing lives in the GCP project."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Google Cloud DNS's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for {name} to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Google Cloud DNS?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in {name}, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
